<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanigalbat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanigalbat was the name used for the reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> state after its defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-sack-of-washukanni">sacked Washukanni</a> around 1340 BCE, Mitanni's territory was confined largely to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> valley. Hanigalbat came under growing Assyrian pressure and was eventually absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, completing the decline of the Mitanni Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanigalbat.jpg" alt="Hanigalbat" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur, Zunkir, rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_du_Mitanni-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
